// SWARM RABBIT HOLE — Wave 9 (Impl-N): Salt ζ, the ADMITTED body —
// transitive closure reach(a) :- edge(a,b), reach(b), end-to-end.
//
// The convergence theorem's sufficient condition (W2C §3.3), made
// executable: a MONOTONE body (in the recursive variable) over a
// FINITE-HEIGHT per-key carrier ⇒ the δ₀/∫ bracket terminates in
// ≤ height rounds at the LEAST fixpoint (Knaster–Tarski; Cousot–Cousot
// Pacific J. Math. 82(1) 1979). Both premises hold for this body:
//   monotone       — adding reached nodes can only add derived nodes;
//   finite-height  — the carrier is presence {0,1} over the keys of
//                    the FINITE edge universe (sets over a finite
//                    universe qualify; ℤ-valued recursion through the
//                    recursive variable does not — that is the
//                    counting-through-recursion rejection, E-ZADM).
//
// Each round is SEMI-NAÏVE (Thm 4b: the incrementalized fixpoint IS
// classical semi-naïve evaluation — Bancilhon–Ramakrishnan SIGMOD
// 1986, derived from the calculus): derive only from the previous
// round's delta δ₀^(r), fold into the integral ∫, then test
// quiescence — Tier G's test is 𝒟(∫α) = 0, the round's delta Z-set
// being empty.
//
// The edge relation is a Z-set of packed pairs: key = a·2³² + b with
// a, b ∈ [0, 2³²) — the same i64 keys the engine's adjacency index
// unpacks. The chain 0→1→…→9 seeded at the far end reaches every node
// in exactly 9 rounds — the ≤ height bound with EQUALITY on a chain
// (the longest derivation chain = the diameter), and the probe count
// is exact: one adjacency probe per round (each round's delta is one
// node, each with one in-neighbor).

fn main() {
    ptr edges = zset_new(0)
    int i = 0
    while (i < 9) {
        zset_add(edges, i * 4294967296 + (i + 1), 1)
        i = i + 1
    }
    ptr seed = zset_new(0)
    zset_add(seed, 9, 1)

    ptr f = zfix_new(edges, seed)
    int rounds = zfix_run(f) // the δ₀/∫ bracket, to quiescence
    print(zfix_len(f))          // 10 — every node reached (the least fixpoint)
    print(rounds)               // 9 — the diameter (≤ height, equality on a chain)
    print(zfix_rounds(f))       // 9 — the round counter readout
    print(zfix_has(f, 0))       // true — the far end of the chain
    print(zfix_has(f, 5))       // true
    print(zfix_has(f, 10))      // false — nothing outside the universe
    print(zfix_probes(f))       // 9 — one adjacency probe per round: semi-naïve
    print(zfix_quiescent_g(f))  // true — 𝒟(∫α) = 0 at the stop

    print("PASS: zfix_closure_chain")
}
